    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Director-of-Growth-Marketing_R0003530": {
      "title": "Director of Growth Marketing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Director of Growth Marketing LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Marketing Communications DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Director of Growth Marketing is a member of WPI's in-house team of experts in marketing strategy, messaging, creative, social media, multimedia, and production management. Under the direction of the AVP of Marketing Communications and in collaboration with campus partners, this team leads WPI's integrated brand strategy and the campaigns that support it. With a heavy focus on student enrollment, the Director of Growth Marketing leads strategy and execution of demand generation initiatives that drive awareness, inquiries, and student pipeline growth across undergraduate and graduate studies and pre-collegiate programs. JOB DESCRIPTION Growth Strategy Lead the university's enrollment growth marketing strategy across the full student acquisition funnel, driving awareness, audience acquisition, inquiries, engagement, and pipeline growth through integrated multichannel campaigns, digital nurture programs, and performance optimization across paid, owned, experiential, and CRM-enabled channels. Develop and lead pre-college engagement and retention strategies that cultivate long-term enrollment pipelines, progressing students from WPI middle school and high school programs toward undergraduate inquiry, application, and enrollment. Develop audience segmentation and targeting approaches for prospective students, families, counselors, educators, and other key enrollment influencers, including guidance counselors, superintendents, STEM coordinators, and STEM-focused student organizations. Drive innovation through experimentation, testing, and evaluation of emerging channels, technologies, and audience engagement strategies to improve enrollment marketing performance. Campaigns, Channels & Execution Lead integrated omnichannel enrollment marketing campaigns. Lead planning, execution, optimization, and performance management of enrollment marketing campaigns in partnership with Creative and Digital Operations teams. Lead strategy for enrollment marketing platforms and third-party lead generation ecosystems (e.g., Niche, CollegeVine, U.S. News & World Report), maximizing visibility, engagement, and ROI. Drive continuous improvement of enrollment marketing assets and campaign experiences in partnership with Creative and Digital Operations teams. Work with partners in Admissions to design and optimize CRM-enabled nurture programs and omnichannel student journeys that improve engagement, conversion, and enrollment outcomes. Experiential & Partnership Marketing Lead recruitment-focused experiential marketing initiatives, including conferences, competitions, trade shows, robotics events, and other strategic activations. Manage external marketing partnerships and sponsorships that support prospective student engagement and enrollment growth. Leadership & Cross-Functional Collaboration Lead a team of two marketing professionals, cultivating talent, collaboration, high performance, and a data-driven culture focused on growth and measurable outcomes. Lead cross-functional planning and execution across Admissions, Marketing Communications, Creative, Digital Operations, university stakeholders, agencies, and vendors to deliver integrated enrollment marketing initiatives and measurable business outcomes. Partner with Undergraduate and Graduate Admissions leadership to align enrollment marketing strategies, recruitment priorities, and institutional enrollment goals. Represent the Marketing team in leadership settings and in interactions with stakeholders within the campus community. Provide direction, prioritization, and performance oversight across enrollment marketing campaigns, initiatives, and investments. In partnership with the Creative Director and aligned with the university brand, ensure consistent application of positioning and messaging across recruitment marketing initiatives. Data, Analytics & Performance Use analytics, attribution, and performance insights to optimize marketing investment and campaign effectiveness. Monitor and report regularly on funnel performance, inquiry growth, campaign ROI, and pipeline contribution. Establish testing frameworks and performance benchmarks across creative, audience targeting, messaging, and conversion strategies. Use AI-enabled tools and predictive insights to improve targeting, personalization, operational efficiency, and enrollment marketing outcomes. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree required. A minimum of eight years of progressive experience in growth marketing, enrollment marketing, demand generation, or related marketing disciplines, including at least three years of supervisory experience. Demonstrated success leading enrollment and growth marketing initiatives that drive measurable awareness, inquiry generation, lead acquisition, engagement, and enrollment pipeline growth. Proven ability to develop and execute data-informed marketing strategies across paid, owned, experiential, CRM-enabled, and marketing automation channels. Experience with CRM and enrollment marketing platforms, including Salesforce, Slate, lead generation ecosystems, marketing automation technologies, audience segmentation, nurture campaigns, and prospective student journey development. Strong analytical, strategic thinking, and problem-solving skills, with experience using analytics, attribution, AI-enabled tools, and performance insights to optimize marketing effectiveness and operational efficiency. Demonstrated ability to influence and lead cross-functional teams and initiatives within complex organizations. Excellent written, verbal, interpersonal, presentation, and stakeholder management skills. Strong project management and organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, budgets, agencies, sponsorships, external partners, and marketing investments. Proven experience in higher education. Agency, consumer, or direct-to-consumer marketing experience preferred. Salary Grade 10: $100,000 – $124,000 per year (depending on experience) Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.  Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Post-Doctoral-Fellow_R0003783": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Post-Doctoral Fellow LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Civil, Environmental, & Architectural Engineering - NFR JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Civil, Environmental, and Architectural Engineering (CEAE) Department at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) is seeking applications for a Post-Doctoral Fellow position. We are looking for a highly motivated, collaborative, and enthusiastic researcher with expertise in an interdisciplinary area involving silica chemistry, biochemistry, and materials science. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal to a 2nd and 3rd year based on performance and funding availability. The probability of a two-year appointment is high assuming strong job performance. JOB DESCRIPTION The lab conducts cutting-edge research in novel, next-generation, multifunctional construction materials. This is a position that requires thinking far outside traditional Civil Engineering boxes, one in which we envision and test ideas for the future of materials that are the most utilized, by mass, on the planet. Our researchers and laboratories maintain collaborations around the country, with leading researchers nationally. We have some of the most advanced experimental and analytical equipment available in any Civil Engineering department in the country, and user facilities and faculty collaborators in other departments as well. This is an unusual opportunity to make a difference. Required Qualifications:   PhD. in a Science or Engineering field directly relevant to materials development and the mechanical and electrical properties of materials. Demonstrated research excellence through  publications  in one or more of the following areas: Novel silica chemistry Crystal dissolution and growth Electrochemical methods coupled to material properties measurement Surface chemistry and associated techniques (interfacial electron transfer, biosensor techniques, scanning probe microscopy techniques, etc.) Computational approaches, if coupled with experimental abilities, including computational chemistry, or machine learning approaches to material discovery and testing. Preferred Qualifications:   Experience conducting research in materials science or closely related areas of expertise. Experience contributing to competitive research grant proposals Experience with innovation in materials development, and intellectual property protection. Experience electrical properties and electrochemical techniques is a plus, but is not required. Ability to work in a team consisting of faculty, other researchers, and graduate students (there is also a possibility of undergraduate researchers) Effective written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to work effectively people in interdisciplinary research.   What We Offer: Opportunities to work on high-impact projects at the intersection of materials science and civil engineering materials. Access to state-of-the-art computational and experimental research facilities unlike any other civil engineering department. Extensive collaborations with leading researchers at other institutions. A supportive research environment including support for career development in academia or industry. Application Instructions: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis and will remain open until the position is filled. Please submit the following materials as a single PDF file:  A brief cover letter outlining your expertise, research background, and research interests  A current curriculum vitae (CV)  Academic transcripts for all completed degrees (Bachelor’s, Master’s, and Ph.D., if applicable)  Full PDF copies of 3-5 representative peer-reviewed journal publications  Contact information for three professional references  Compensation: Salary: $65,000 - $70,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity. WPI’s  benefits package  includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Assistant-Professor_R0003517": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of AI in Mechatronics and Controls",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Professor of AI in Mechatronics and Controls LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Mechanical & Materials Engineering DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Mechanical and Materials Engineering Department at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) seeks applicants for a tenure-track faculty position in the area of Applied Artificial Intelligence (AI) for Mechatronics and Controls available at the assistant professor level. JOB DESCRIPTION The successful candidate will possess a strong background in Mechatronics and Controls, demonstrate proficiency in applying AI techniques to these application areas, and strengthen the connection with the Robotics Engineering Department.  Expertise in intelligent sensing and perception, cyber-physical systems, data-driven modeling and control, and AI/deep learning is essential. Applications may include autonomous systems and mobility, intelligent infrastructure, human–robot interaction, smart health, advanced manufacturing and Industry 4.0/5.0, mechatronics for sustainability, and other areas complementary to the department’s existing strengths. A proven ability to integrate multidisciplinary methodologies to solve complex challenges is highly desirable.  The candidate will be expected to develop an active externally funded research program and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in mechanical engineering. Candidate is required to have a PhD or equivalent degree in mechanical engineering, robotics engineering, or another relevant discipline.   This position may be appointed to 25% or a more suitable fraction in the Robotics Engineering Department, as agreed by the successful candidate and the two departments. This position is expected to be filled at the Assistant Professor level. For experienced candidates, rank will be commensurate with experience. The Mechanical and Materials Engineering Department at WPI (https://www.wpi.edu/academics/departments/mechanical-materials-engineering) has 27 dual-mission faculty, 9 full-time teaching faculty, and 12 research faculty.  MME offers undergraduate degrees in mechanical engineering to over 1100 students. The department offers graduate degrees in mechanical engineering, materials science and engineering, and manufacturing engineering to about 200 graduate students. The department’s annual externally sponsored research expenditures have exceeded $ 16M . WPI Robotics Engineering Department ( www.wpi.edu/+RBE ) is the first-of-its-kind department in the nation with a vision to be an exemplary multidisciplinary center of education, research, and innovation in robotics. It is built on an ABET-accredited Robotics Engineering program. RBE counts ~800 undergraduate and graduate students and offers robotics engineering BS, MS, and Ph.D. degrees. In 2016 the Robotics Engineering program was awarded the inaugural ABET Innovation Award for its truly interdisciplinary, project-based curriculum. The department has 23 faculty members and 20+ affiliated faculty members. Our faculty has research support from NSF, NIH, AFRL, Army, ONR, NASA, DARPA, and industry funding. We expect the new faculty member to contribute to the exceptionally close-knit, collaborative, and collegial atmosphere we currently enjoy WPI’s reputation as a rigorous and innovative university rests on the shoulders of its faculty. A highly selective, private technological university and one of the nation’s first, WPI believes that when great minds work together, great advances follow. At WPI the boundaries to multidisciplinary collaboration are low - faculty members, students, and other partners work together on the real-world projects and purposeful research that are hallmarks of the WPI experience. Located one hour west of Boston, the university’s campus is in Worcester, Massachusetts, a thriving 21st century college city recognized as a growing hub of scientific and technological innovation. Applications should include curriculum vitae, statements of teaching and research interests, and a list of five professional references submitted . This search will remain open until the position is filled. Questions can be addressed to the Chair of the Search Committee, Professor Zhu Mao, zmao2@wpi.edu .   Compensation: $105,000 – $118,000 per year. Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Director--Systems-Engineering_R0003675": {
      "title": "Director, Systems Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Director, Systems Engineering LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Infrastructure Services and Architecture DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Reporting to the Assistant Chief Technology Officer, Infrastructure Services and Architecture, the Director of Systems Engineering will lead the team responsible for the university's servers, storage, cloud computing, and virtualization, along with infrastructure DevOps, application system and security support, and the technology behind Campus Safety Services (card access, video surveillance, emergency radios, and emergency phones). WPI's information technology environment is highly distributed and diverse. This senior leadership role combines strategic vision and planning with direct, hands-on technical involvement alongside the engineers who report to the role. The Director is responsible for the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the systems that support the university's educational and operational objectives, and for the reliability of the systems that support campus safety. This position is hybrid, with 4 days on campus in Worcester and 1 day from home. JOB DESCRIPTION Strategic Leadership and Team Development Develop and implement a comprehensive systems strategy aligned with the university's goals and objectives. Collaborate with the Assistant CTO and other senior leaders to prioritize systems initiatives and align them with institutional priorities. Develop staff, set clear performance expectations, and manage workload and operational tasks across the systems team. Work with other groups within IT and other business units to develop solutions for unique system requirements, including technical and regulatory requirements. Systems, Storage, Virtualization, and Cloud Engineering Working directly with the Systems Engineers who report to this role, direct the design, implementation, and management of the university's physical and virtual server infrastructure, enterprise storage and backup platforms, and AWS and Azure cloud environments. Participate actively in solution design, technical review, and implementation planning alongside the engineering team. Ensure efficient use of resources through effective virtualization, capacity planning, and lifecycle management. Lead the evaluation, selection, and adoption of new platform technologies. Ensure new implementations are documented, supportable, and aligned with institutional standards and architecture. Systems Operations and Administration Oversee the team that performs the day-to-day operation and administration of the university's systems environment, ensuring stability, availability, and performance. Ensure all key services, systems, and components are under appropriate monitoring and alerting. Ensure operating system patching, firmware maintenance, and lifecycle activities are performed on a defined and predictable cadence. Ensure documentation, asset inventory, and configuration records are up to date and accurate. Serve as the senior escalation point during major incidents, directing the response, restoring service, and driving root cause analysis and preventive action to completion. Ensure systems incidents and service requests are resolved within the applicable SLA, maintaining open communication with other ITS teams, including the Service Desk. DevOps, Automation, and Process Improvement Manage the DevOps resource supporting the infrastructure organization, directing the work against strategic DevOps and automation requirements from the Assistant CTO. Identify repeated work and recurring requests across all areas reporting to this role. Define, document, and standardize the processes behind that work so it can be measured and performed consistently by both full-time employees and the managed service provider. Identify and prioritize the automation opportunities with the greatest return on team efficiency, and direct their design, build, and adoption. Measure the effect of automation and process improvement on toil, cycle time, and error rates, and adjust priorities based on results. Application System and Security Support Own the systems and middleware layer beneath the university's enterprise and departmental applications, including database platforms, web services, and load balancing. Ensure these platforms are appropriately sized, patched, monitored, hardened, and recoverable. Partner with application owners and the Office of Information Security to remediate vulnerabilities and apply security controls at the system and middleware layer. Coordinate maintenance windows and upgrade planning with application stakeholders to minimize disruption to academic and administrative operations. Campus Safety Services Oversee the technology, operation, and support of campus safety systems, including physical card access, video surveillance, emergency radios, and the emergency phone system. Ensure the security, reliability, and availability of these physical security and life-safety systems. Oversee deployment, upgrade, and lifecycle planning for campus safety technology, including coverage associated with building renovation and construction projects. Collaborate with Campus Police, Facilities, and external vendors and integrators to align technology capability with campus safety requirements. Security, Compliance, and Resilience Ensure all systems components meet institutional security and compliance requirements, applying best practices across all infrastructure reporting to this role. Ensure system designs and procedures are aligned with, and capable of enforcing, university policies and best practices. Ensure backup, recovery, and business continuity capabilities are designed, documented, and regularly tested against defined recovery objectives. Ensure vulnerabilities are remediated within institutional service level targets, and provide the Information Security operations team with the access and tooling needed to identify, locate, and isolate systems involved in security incidents. Support audit activity and the production of control evidence, and assist Information Security in drafting policies that overlap with the systems team. Budget, Vendor, and Resource Management Develop and manage the operating and capital budget for the systems team and campus safety technology. Identify opportunities for cost savings and license optimization. Oversee vendor relationships and negotiate contracts for systems, storage, cloud, and campus safety services and products, working directly with vendors, resellers, and integrators. Plan and forecast refresh and renewal cycles to support multi-year budget development. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in Computer Science, Engineering, Information Systems, Information Technology, or a related field, or equivalent experience. Minimum of 7 years of experience, including direct leadership of a systems, infrastructure, or operations team. Strong leadership and team management skills, with the ability to develop and retain technical staff. Excellent strategic thinking and planning abilities. Deep, hands-on understanding of server, storage, virtualization, and cloud computing technologies across both Windows and Linux environments. Working knowledge of the middleware layer supporting enterprise applications, including database platforms, web services, and load balancing. Experience directing DevOps and automation work. Knowledge of physical access control, video surveillance, and emergency communications systems. Strong problem-solving and decision-making capabilities, including the ability to serve as a senior escalation point during major incidents. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Experience developing and managing operating and capital budgets and negotiating with vendors. PREFERRED CERTIFICATIONS Certifications are preferred, not required, and no candidate is expected to hold all of them. Credentials relevant to WPI's technology stack include: Windows Server: Microsoft Certified: Windows Server Administrator Associate (Exam AZ-802), or the predecessor Windows Server Hybrid Administrator Associate (Exams AZ-800 and AZ-801). Cloud platforms: Microsoft Certified: Azure Administrator Associate (AZ-104) or Azure Solutions Architect Expert (AZ-305); AWS Certified SysOps Administrator - Associate or AWS Certified Solutions Architect. Red Hat Enterprise Linux: Red Hat Certified System Administrator (RHCSA, EX200) or Red Hat Certified Engineer (RHCE); the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Deployment and Systems Management (EX403) additionally covers Red Hat Satellite. Nutanix: Nutanix Certified Professional - Multicloud Infrastructure (NCP-MCI), or Nutanix Certified Master - Multicloud Infrastructure (NCM-MCI). NetApp: NetApp Certified Data Administrator, ONTAP (NCDA), or a NetApp Certified Implementation Engineer (NCIE) specialty such as Data Protection or SAN. Rubrik: Rubrik Certified System Administrator (RCSA). Salary Grade 11 : $109,000 – $140,000 per year, depending on experience. Benefits include: 9.5% retirement match 4 weeks of paid time off, plus the university's December closure Free tuition to WPI Health insurance, flexible spending accounts, and work-life programs Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.  Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Teaching-Professor-of-Studio-Arts_R0003790": {
      "title": "Teaching Professor of Studio Arts",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Teaching Professor of Studio Arts LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Humanities & Arts DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Department of Humanities and Arts at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) invites applications for a Teaching Professor of Studio Arts (non-tenure track) at the rank of Assistant or Associate, beginning in August 2027. The Teaching Professor track at WPI is a full-time faculty position and affords the possibility of reappointment and promotion. We seek candidates grounded in traditional studio art practices who also engage with contemporary technological tools and methods. We are looking for an excellent teacher whose primary responsibilities include instructing courses for first- and second-year students, such as Essentials of Art and Topics in Studio Art, as well as more advanced classes in areas such as figurative studies and thematic inquiry. Experience in curriculum development is preferred, as there will be opportunities to rethink our current offerings and develop new ones based on student needs and faculty expertise. The position also involves establishing and cultivating relationships with local art organizations and institutions. Activities will take place in our newly opened art studio, so candidates should be able to help supervise and manage facilities and programming. We seek those who prioritize accessibility, sustainability, health, and safety. JOB DESCRIPTION Job responsibilities: Teach 8 courses per academic year: two courses per 7-week term (2 terms per semester) Advise student projects  Contribute to the initiatives of the Humanities and Arts Department and the University.  Qualifications:   MFA or Ph.D in Studio Arts or a closely related field Three years of successful classroom teaching and advising experience at the university level  Record of exhibition and/or projects including exhibition development experience Skills teaching a variety of studio media As part of your application, please submit:   Cover letter that describes interests, values, and practices as an artist, scholar, and teacher  CV that includes academic and professional experience, as well as the names and contact information of three professional references   A statement of teaching philosophy A PDF portfolio of professional work includes 15-20 high-quality images with information about each work including title, date, size, and medium (limit PDF to 30 mb)  A PDF portfolio of student works of 15-20 high-quality images with information about each work including date, size, medium, and a short description of each assignment (limit PDF to 30mb).    We will request letters of reference, writing samples, and a full teaching portfolio for short-listed candidates only.  Application review will begin October 19 and will continue until filled. WPI  Worcester Polytechnic Institute, founded in 1865, is one of the nation’s oldest technological universities. WPI’s mission is to create and convey scientific and technical knowledge in ways that benefit society. Education in the humanities and arts, which includes history, is central to this societal connection. The Humanities and Arts Department is home to more than ten historians and includes more than sixty faculty in Philosophy, Religion, Literature, Languages, Writing, Music, Theatre and Art. WPI encourages collaborations not only within multidisciplinary departments like HUA but throughout the university. Faculty members, students, and other partners work together on real-world projects and purposeful research that are hallmarks of the WPI experience. We are proud of our No. 1 ranking for “faculty who best combine research and teaching” (Wall Street Journal/Times Higher Ed, 2016).  Located one hour west of Boston, the university’s campus is in Worcester, Massachusetts, a thriving 21st-century college city recognized as a growing hub of scientific and technological innovation.   Salary: $85,000 - $88,000. Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity. WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more. FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Assistant-Teaching-Professor-of-Music_R0003792": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Music-Concert Band",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Teaching Professor of Music-Concert Band LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Humanities & Arts DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The music division at WPI seeks a Director of Wind Band Activities at the level of Assistant Teaching Professor (full time, non-tenure-track) to begin in the Fall of 2027. Applicants must be prepared to lead the WPI Concert Band and a variety of small music ensembles; teach courses in the core music curriculum as well as specialized courses in their area of interest; and serve as an advisor on student projects and clubs. The position requires teaching excellence, organizational and leadership skills, strong mentorship skills, flexibility, and cultural sensitivity. In their application materials, candidates should demonstrate the ability to teach engaging, project-based courses; to mentor students of mixed experience levels in performance ensembles; and to contribute to creating a positive and inclusive culture for all students. JOB DESCRIPTION About Music at WPI: As a division of the Department of Humanities & Arts, Music at WPI supports numerous choral and instrumental ensembles that students may participate in for credit, while also offering a wide range of classes in theory and composition, performance, music history, technology and science, and music and culture. The WPI Concert Band typically numbers between 70-100 students and functions simultaneously as a course, bearing academic credit through the Department of Humanities and Arts, and as a student club. Rehearsals are held twice weekly for the full ensemble with sectionals frequently being held during the week. The Concert Band typically performs four major concerts per year, plus additional smaller programs, both on campus and off campus, and regularly tours internationally. Students may study music to satisfy the Humanities and Arts requirement and may also continue to pursue a Minor or a Major Concentration in Music. Position Expectations: The teaching load for this position is the equivalent of eight courses per year—which includes all the following activities: Performance responsibilities: Concert Band Small Ensembles Teaching responsibilities: Four academic music classes, one per term Student club advising responsibilities: Faculty advisor of the Wind Band Association Additional Responsibilities : Organize and lead the WPI Concert Band in regular rehearsals and concerts. Advise and coach smaller ensembles derived from the Wind Band Association such as Clarinet Quartet, Saxophone Quintet, and Flute Ensemble. There is also both an established Brass Ensemble and Percussion Ensemble, which may fall under the purview of this position depending on the director’s background. Work with the student officers in the Wind Band Association to manage the regular activities and musical performances of the Concert Band and smaller ensembles. Organize and facilitate national and international performance tours with the Concert Band. Ensure proper maintenance and organization of the band equipment. Teach music courses and seminars as assigned. This may include introductory courses in music appreciation and theory, and a practicum in music performance. Design and revise the music curriculum in collaboration with the other music faculty. Participate in initiatives conducted by the Humanities and Arts Department. Advise student projects. Work with WPI’s Advancement Office and Alumni Relations Office to manage regular giving and alumni focused activities This is a 9-month faculty position, allowing for both external and internal opportunities (such as professional development, research, or additional teaching) during the summer months. This position is on WPI's “teaching track,” which provides secure long-term contracts, with opportunities for promotion. Candidate Qualifications: Doctorate in Music. A highly qualified candidate with a Master's degree may be considered for appointment at the Instructor level. Academic focus on instrumental music. Successful experience directing bands at the university level. Application Materials: As part of your application, please submit:  Cover letter that addresses your teaching experience; your professional work; how you would teach the courses described above at WPI; and how you see yourself adding to WPI’s efforts to build a strong and positive learning environment for all students Teaching statement A link to a video (10-15 min) of you conducting a concert band in performance and / or rehearsal (both are preferred) CV, including academic and professional experience We will request letters of reference and additional materials for short-listed candidates only.  Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received by November 1, 2026 will receive full consideration. About WPI Worcester Polytechnic Institute, founded in 1865, is one of the nation’s oldest technological universities located in Central Massachusetts. WPI’s mission is to create and convey scientific and technical knowledge in ways that benefit society. Education in the humanities and arts, which includes language, is central to this societal connection. The Humanities and Arts Department is home to more than sixty faculty in Philosophy, Religion, Literature, Languages, Writing, Music, Theatre and Art. WPI encourages collaborations not only within multidisciplinary departments like HUA but throughout the university. Faculty members, students, and other partners work together on real-world projects and purposeful research that are hallmarks of the WPI experience. Located one hour west of Boston, the university campus is in Worcester, Massachusetts, a thriving 21st-century college city recognized as a growing hub of scientific and technological innovation. Salary: $78,000 - $80,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Practice-Point-Technical-Operations-Manager_R0003791": {
      "title": "Practice Point Technical Operations Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Practice Point Technical Operations Manager LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Biomanufacturing Education and Training Center - BETC DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Technical Operations Manager (TFM) of PracticePoint is responsible for the proper functioning of all aspects of the facility and for technical innovation, enabling the fulfillment of its mission of supporting research and development across the breadth of medical technology. The TFM will also serve as the Director of the Medical Engineering and Imaging Core (MEIC), a core facility that supports WPI faculty and students in efficiently using the equipment in PracticePoint. The TFM will work closely with the PracticePoint Director and staff to ensure that the facilities are equipped and operating for sustainable success, with appropriate plans for maintenance and upgrades. The TFM will participate, as appropriate, in the research and development work carried out in the facility as well as in outreach and identification of new members and facility users. The TFM must be qualified, or become qualified, to operate the MRI machine, fluoroscope, surgical lasers, and other sophisticated and potentially hazardous equipment. The TFM is responsible for the overall safe operation of the facility. JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities: Manage MEIC operations. Work with faculty and students on facility use scheduling, technical support, financial arrangements (billing and invoicing), and facility equipment maintenance. Research potential members, conduct technically oriented facility tours, and plan clients' use of the facilities. Work with members, potential members, and other users to obtain maximum effectiveness from use of the facilities. This requires in-depth knowledge of the capabilities of the equipment as well as knowledge of the healthcare areas being studied. Participate in PracticePoint dry and wet lab events, including pre-lab setup and preparation, intra-lab support, and post-lab cleaning and follow-up. Work with the Director of PracticePoint, PracticePoint staff, and PracticePoint-affiliated faculty to conduct research on novel medical technologies in robotics, AI, imaging, and materials. Maintain oversight of the facilities with regard to maintenance and upgrades so that the facility continues to meet the needs of WPI researchers and external members. Obtain and maintain appropriate training credentials and approvals for operation of all PracticePoint equipment, including the MRI, fluoroscope, lasers, and machine shops, together with appropriate credentials for training other users and operators. Oversee the delivery and use of all hazardous materials, and maintain currency with safety standards and regulatory requirements for all aspects of the facility. Maintain close coordination with the Environmental Health and Safety Office as well as relevant committees, including the IRB, IBC, and IACUC. Have and oversee access to all areas of the facility, based on either card or physical key access. Oversee, with the Business Manager, facility scheduling and charge rates for internal and external users. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Master's or PhD degree in Biomedical or Medical Robotics Engineering or a closely related area such as Biophysics or Life Science required. PhD preferred. Two or more years of hands-on experience with medical imaging equipment operations (MRI, C-arm, ultrasound, etc.). Previous research experience in Biomedical or Medical Robotics areas preferred. Excellent knowledge of MS Office, databases, and information systems. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Ability to work independently to accomplish the position requirements, while also being effective as part of the PracticePoint team. Strong creativity and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated experience in planning and plan execution. Salary Grade 8: $71,800 – $90,474 per year (depending on experience) Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.  Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Financial-Specialist-Global-School_R0003788": {
      "title": "Financial Specialist Global School",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Financial Specialist Global School LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME The Global School DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY This position is responsible for the budgeting, tracking, and auditing of our Global School project centers. The Fiscal Specialist will work closely with GEO staff, faculty program directors, campus partners, and university offices to support accurate, timely, and compliant fiscal processes. This role contributes to the successful planning and delivery of our Global Projects Program and the long-term sustainability of the centers. JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities: Advise and guide study abroad program directors on the development of Study Abroad program budgets: Share responsibility for supporting 60+ project center directors in the creation and management of individual program budgets, ranging from approximately $30,000-$200,000 in total costs. Provide guidance to program directors on fiscal policies, procedures, timelines, budget development, program fees, best practices, and compliance with federal, state, WPI, and Global School guidelines. Support financial trainings for new and returning advisors and directors in individual, group, and virtual settings. Monitoring spending and alerting leadership to any anomalies or opportunities for cost savings. Manage fiscal processes for Global School budget portfolio: Share responsibility for managing fiscal processes for an assigned portfolio of short-term and semester-long study abroad programs within a ~$7.5 million operation. Manage Workday workflow, hiring paperwork, onboarding support, timesheets, pay periods, and payments for study abroad faculty, program assistants, and student support. Reconcile payroll transactions and coordinate funds recovery based on expense types and allocations. Process payments for and monitor billing of international health insurance. Process study abroad travel, including travel authorizations, pre-approvals, transportation requests, reimbursements, and related account transactions. Authorize stateside spending for program-related expenses, including credit card, ACH, vendor payments, registrations, supplies, and other approved purchasing methods. Review vendor contracts and agreements and coordinate timely payments in accordance with university and Global School procedures. Maintain financial records, spreadsheets, documentation, and summary analyses for reporting, forecasting, and decision-making. Manage fiscal processes internal to the Global School: Process financial transactions related to domestic and international travel for the Global Projects Program in compliance with WPI travel policies and procedures. Complete hiring paperwork, PAPs, timesheets, and payroll processing for wage and student employees in the unit. Reconcile monthly credit card transactions, prepare financial documents, and maintain organized records. Provide fiscal support related to events and meetings, internal purchasing, office supplies, promotional items, mailings, and other administrative processes. Build relationships with internal partners, including Finance, Talent and Inclusion, Financial Aid, and other university offices, to support smooth processing of mission-critical financial functions. Apply federal, state, university, and Global School financial policies and procedures consistently to ensure fiscal activity is completed accurately and in compliance. Requirements: Bachelor's degree Minimum two years of relevant experience Fiscal, budget, accounting, procurement, travel, or reconciliation experience in higher education or a similarly regulated environment. Experience using financial, travel, purchasing, student information, or related business systems to support transactions, reconciliations, payroll, hiring, or other business processes. Ability to effectively and professionally communicate with partners across multiple departments in individual and group settings. Interpersonal skills and demonstrated ability to work with broad groups of students, faculty, staff, and campus partners. Working knowledge of Workday and Microsoft Office Suite, including Word, Excel, Outlook, and Teams. Attention to detail, resourcefulness, organization, and effective time management. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and maintain accurate records. Ability to work independently while also contributing effectively in a team environment. Ability to apply policies, procedures, and guidelines consistently and use sound judgment when completing detailed administrative or fiscal tasks. Interest in international education, study abroad, global engagement, or financial operations in higher education. Compensation: Salary: $60,-000 - $65,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
