    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Assistant-Teaching-Professor-in-Chinese_R0003786": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Chinese",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Teaching Professor in Chinese LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Humanities & Arts DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Department of Humanities and Arts at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure track teaching faculty position in Chinese to begin in August 2027. While not tenure-eligible, this position is renewable and offers a path to secure continuing contracts. We are seeking a dedicated language educator who can work both collaboratively and independently and also contribute to WPI’s Global Asia initiatives. The ideal candidate is adaptable, thrives in a dynamic academic environment, and is committed to ongoing professional growth. WPI operates on a distinctive seven-week term schedule, consisting of four terms per academic year. The hired faculty member will teach 8 courses per year (2 courses per term). JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities:   Teach 8 courses per academic year: two courses per 7-week term    Deliver effective, engaging, and inclusive Chinese language instruction to undergraduate students.   Work both collaboratively and independently, contributing to broader departmental and institutional initiatives.   Practice innovative pedagogical approaches and deliver instruction based on established best practices.   Qualifications:    Education & Credentials: A Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Teaching Chinese as a Foreign Language, Second Language Acquisition or a related field is preferred. A highly qualified candidate with a Master's degree may be considered for appointment at the Instructor level. Formal training in teaching Chinese as a foreign language and/or second language acquisition/language education is required. Language:  Native or near-native proficiency in Mandarin Chinese and professional-level proficiency in English are required.   Teaching Experience:  A minimum of 5 years of experience teaching Chinese at multiple levels at the college level in the United States is required, as well as familiarity with a variety of teaching methods.   Professional Qualities:  Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and independently, a willingness to adapt and learn, and a clear record of professional growth.   Application Instructions:   To apply, please submit the following application materials:   A cover letter addressing your qualifications and interest in the position.   A current Curriculum Vitae (CV).   Two sample syllabi: one for an Elementary Chinese course, one of the candidate’s choosing   Please submit only the materials listed above. Short-listed candidates may be contacted to submit additional materials, including a teaching portfolio with a statement of teaching philosophy, a 20 - 40-minute teaching demonstration video, and three letters of reference (one of which must be from a teaching supervisor). Application review will begin October 19 and will continue until filled. Salary: $78,000 - $82,000. Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity. WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more. FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Student-Officer-in-Training--Entry-Level-OK-_R0003784": {
      "title": "Student Officer in Training (Entry Level OK)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Student Officer in Training (Entry Level OK) LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Campus Safety DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY JOB DESCRIPTION The Student Officer in Training is WPI's paid training pathway to becoming a full-time Campus Police Officer. Candidates selected for this program will be hired by WPI into a temporary, full-time, non-sworn position with the WPI Police Department and sponsored by WPI to attend the Municipal Police Training Committee (MPTC) Police Academy in Marlborough, MA. WPI will provide the required training pathway and pay Student Officers in Training $25 per hour on a full-time basis while they attend the academy and complete required departmental training. Upon successful graduation from the police academy, the employee will transition directly into a full-time, sworn Campus Police Officer position with the WPI Police Department. At that time, compensation will increase to the Campus Police Officer starting rate under the WPI Campus Police union contract. The anticipated training appointment runs from November 16, 2026, through graduation from the police academy on April 23, 2027. During this period, Student Officers in Training will attend the academy and complete required WPI Police Department training in preparation for their transition to the sworn Campus Police Officer role. Responsibilities Attend and successfully complete the MPTC Recruit Officer Course, including all academic, physical fitness, firearms, and skills components, and meet all attendance and conduct requirements of the academy. Complete the WPI Police Department dispatch and field training programs. Assist WPI Police staff by performing safety and security inspections of campus buildings and grounds and backfilling the position of WPI Police Dispatcher. Provide public safety-related services, including escorts and lockout assistance, to members of and visitors to the WPI community. Render assistance to those in need and make referrals to campus resources as appropriate. Provide information to the public and community members while promoting positive relations between the department and the community. Respond to calls for police service in a support capacity. Demonstrate the ability to analyze emergency situations and assist sworn officers in developing incident action plans. Conduct initial fact-gathering for emergency incidents and submit written reports, as required. Perform traffic control and parking enforcement. Report violations of University policies related to safety and security concerns. Collaborate with WPI community members to enhance public safety, with specific emphasis on crime prevention and awareness. Act as a Campus Security Authority pursuant to the Clery Act. Perform all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications High school diploma or GED required. Associate degree in Criminal Justice or a related field preferred. Must be at least 21 years of age at the time of application. Must possess a valid driver's license at the time of appointment. Must be of good moral character and fit for employment in law enforcement. Must be eligible to obtain a Massachusetts License to Carry Firearms prior to the firearms segment of the academy. Must be able to obtain First Aid and CPR certifications. Must be physically fit and able to pass the MPTC pre-entrance physical fitness assessment (Cooper standards) and successfully complete the academy. Must successfully complete an oral interview, a background investigation conducted by WPI Police, state and national criminal background checks, a CORI check, and a medical examination, including any examinations and drug testing required for academy admission. Must successfully complete a psychological evaluation meeting Massachusetts Peace Officer Standards and Training (POST) Commission standards upon conditional offer. Must be able to obtain POST certification and an SSPO warrant of appointment upon graduation from the academy. Must be able to work all hours and in varied weather conditions, including nights, weekends, holidays, and overtime as assigned. For any week of academy training that is less than 40 hours, the Student Officer in Training must report to the WPI Police Department to fulfill the 40-hour-per-week commitment. Must be able to maintain professionalism during periods of stress. Must be able to physically perform the essential functions of the position. Continued employment is contingent upon successful completion of the police academy. Failure to successfully complete the academy will result in termination of the Student Officer in Training appointment. Additional Information Student Officer in Training: $25.00 per hour during academy attendance and departmental training. Upon academy graduation and appointment as a sworn Campus Police Officer, compensation will increase to the applicable starting rate established by the WPI Campus Police union contract. FLSA STATUS United States of America (Non-Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Head-Coach-of-Softball-WPE-Instructor_R0003780": {
      "title": "Head Coach of Softball/WPE Instructor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Head Coach of Softball/WPE Instructor LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Physical Education, Recreation & Athletics - PERA DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Responsible for providing leadership and direction for all aspects of the Softball program as they compete competitively in NCAA Division III athletics while providing an educational environment which enhances the development of the whole student-athlete. Additional responsibilities include teaching courses in physical education as well as departmental administrative responsibilities. JOB DESCRIPTION Provide leadership and direction to the Softball program; create a culture of compliance with NCAA rules and regulations as well as New England Women's and Men's Athletic Conference (NEWMAC) and WPI university policies and procedures. Develop team and individual strategies to create an environment of success through effective teaching of technical and fundamental softball skills. Responsible for direct supervision of assistant coach(es), and any other personnel management duties as required to maintain a successful staff. Recruit prospective student-athletes committed to academic and athletic excellence; establish ways to ensure success for all team members academically, athletically, and socially. Maintain a strong connection with the WPI softball alumni group through communication and relationship development. Responsible for successfully managing the program's operating and fundraising budgets. Teach physical education courses and perform other administrative duties as assigned. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree required. 3 to 5 years of collegiate coaching experience. Salary Grade (7): $60,000 to $70,000 (depending on experience) Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.  FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
