    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Adjunct-Instructor---Biomanufacturing_R0003417": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor – Biomanufacturing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-04-24\nPart time\n\nJOB TITLE Adjunct Instructor – Biomanufacturing LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Biomanufacturing Education and Training Center - BETC - JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Biomanufacturing Education & Training Center (BETC) at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) is seeking to expand its pool of adjunct instructors to support hands-on training programs in its state-of-the-art, pilot-scale mammalian biomanufacturing facility. BETC is the first center in New England to offer intensive, immersive education and workforce training for professionals currently working in the biotechnology and biopharmaceutical industries, as well as individuals seeking to transition into biomanufacturing careers. We are looking for experienced subject matter experts who are passionate about sharing industry knowledge and training the next generation of biomanufacturing talent. JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities: Adjunct instructors teach onsite at the BETC facility and facilitate both classroom instruction and hands-on laboratory training. Instructors will guide participants through core concepts and real‑world applications across the biomanufacturing workflow. Specifically, we are seeking subject matter experts in one or more of the following areas: Upstream Processing of Mammalian (Animal) Cell Cultures Cell culture fundamentals Bioreactor operations Process monitoring and optimization Downstream Processing Principles and Techniques Harvest, clarification, and purification Chromatography and filtration Process understanding within a GMP framework Instructional assignments are flexible and may include teaching in a specific technical area or across multiple modules, depending on expertise. Evening and flexible scheduling options are available to accommodate industry professionals. Requirements: Minimum of 5 years of professional experience working in the bioprocessing or biomanufacturing industry Demonstrated ability to operate laboratory and bioprocessing equipment in a simulated GMP manufacturing environment Prior teaching, training, or on-the-job mentoring experience preferred Strong organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills Ability to effectively engage adult learners with diverse professional backgrounds Additional Details: This is a part-time, on-site adjunct teaching opportunity (contracted position) All instruction is conducted onsite at the BETC facility Course schedules are offered throughout the year and can be designed to align with instructor availability To Apply: Please attach a resume for consideration. Compensation: This position pays $75 per hour + depending on experience FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Adjunct-Faculty-Department-of-Integrative-and-Global-Studies_R0002267": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty-Department of Integrative and Global Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-10-27\nPart time\n\nJOB TITLE Adjunct Faculty-Department of Integrative and Global Studies LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Integrative & Global Studies - JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) is seeking part-time adjunct faculty to teach evening classes for the Department of Integrative and Global Studies. These faculty positions are created to provide an available applicant pool for the program. Positions will be filled on an as needed basis. Start dates will vary by course and semester. Qualified applicants will be contacted by the program. Your application will remain in the pool until the posting closes. WPI is a diverse campus of learners passionate about creating an inclusive workplace that promotes and values diversity. We are looking for candidates who can support our commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion. JOB DESCRIPTION The Department of Integrative and Global Studies (DIGS) at WPI has a continuing need for adjunct professors to teach a unique course in social methods. In this class, students to work in teams to prepare a research proposal to guide work for external sponsors in the following term.  Students must learn to function in teams to set goals, complete background research, understand appropriate research methods, think critically, and write and present effectively.  Experience in social research and in teaching social research methods is highly desirable.  A Ph.D. in a relevant social science discipline is also desirable, but not required. The Global Experience Office (GEO) manages WPI’s Global Perspective Program through which most of WPI’s juniors complete interdisciplinary projects off campus each year.  In this project, the Interactive Qualifying Project, student teams work with external sponsors on a problem addressing some aspect of science, technology and human needs, broadly defined.  For more information on the program, please see our website at http://www.wpi.edu/academics/GPP/index.html . The WPI calendar is based on four 7-week terms, two in the fall and two in the spring.  Our project preparation course is offered only in the evenings, Mondays & Thursdays, 6:30 – 8:30 p.m. for seven weeks each. Instructors must be available at other times for office hours and consultation with students. Compensation: $6,000 per ID2050 course and $12,000 Global Projects Program Advisor FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Adjunct-Faculty-Off-Campus-Advisor_R0002911": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty-Off-Campus Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-10-27\nPart time\n\nJOB TITLE Adjunct Faculty-Off-Campus Advisor LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Integrative & Global Studies - JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Worcester Polytechnic Institute's Global School is seeking adjunct faculty to advise project teams as part of WPI’s signature Global Projects Program. Successful candidates will receive a 2 month, destination specific appointment completed full-time onsite at a project center. Project center destinations include international and domestic locations around the world. This onsite position also requires part-time advising in the 2 months prior to departure (completed on-campus or remotely) as well as mandatory participation in advisor trainings. These faculty positions are created to provide an available applicant pool for the program. Positions will be filled on an as needed basis. Start dates will vary by project center. Qualified applicants will be contacted by the program. Your application will remain in the pool until the posting closes. If you wish to be considered for positions beyond that date, then you must reapply. JOB DESCRIPTION Advisors work with students completing the Interactive Qualifying Project (IQP) at WPI’s project center destinations around the world. The IQP is one of the most distinctive elements of the WPI Plan and WPI’s signature project-based curriculum. Unlike an academic course, this nine-credit-hour requirement involves students working in teams, with students not in their major, to tackle an issue that relates science, engineering, and technology to society. Advisors guide students through their project experience while also providing student support onsite. Advisors are asked to lead onsite orientations, respond to emergencies, and assist project teams to resolve issues of group dynamics, sponsor relationships, or other issues that arise. These highly demanding positions require a significant amount of flexibility, self-initiative, and the ability to excel under challenging conditions while efficiently accomplishing tasks in a fast-paced environment. An ideal applicant will hold an advanced degree in a related discipline with relevant practical, in addition to successful undergraduate or graduate teaching experience. Experience living or working abroad is also highly desired. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, and names and contact information for three references. The cover letter should feature the candidate's relevant teaching experience and area(s) of scholarly interest. Compensation: $3,000 per PQP and $12,000 Global Projects Program Advisor FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Adjunct-Teaching-Faculty--Biomedical-Engineering_R0002043": {
      "title": "Adjunct Teaching Faculty | Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-10-27\nPart time\n\nJOB TITLE Adjunct Teaching Faculty | Biomedical Engineering LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Biomedical Engineering - JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY An ideal applicant will hold an advanced degree in Biomedical Engineering or related discipline with significant practical experience in the relevant area, as well as having successful undergraduate or graduate teaching experience. Abilities to teach in other areas is also valued. JOB DESCRIPTION Worcester Polytechnic Institute is seeking part-time adjunct faculty to teach day and/or evening classes for the Biomedical Engineering Department, at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Part-time faculty positions are created to provide an available applicant pool for the department. Positions will be filled on an as needed basis. Start dates will vary by course and semester. Qualified applicants will be contacted by the academic department when an opening is anticipated. Your application will remain in the pool until the posting closes. If you wish to be considered for positions beyond that date you must reapply.   Responsibilities include teaching from established curriculum but with addition of personal expertise and experience; provide students with timely feedback; grading. Prior experience teaching college-level courses required. Applicants should review the WPI course catalog and identify engineering courses they may be qualified to teach. Experience with online teaching and the Canvas learning management system preferred but not required. PhD in related discipline preferred; master’s degree in related discipline required. Compensation: $7,000–$8,500 per course FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Adjunct-Openings---School-of-Business_R0001028": {
      "title": "Adjunct Openings - School of Business",
      "description": "Posted: 2025-10-27\nPart time\n\nJOB TITLE Adjunct Openings - School of Business LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME The Business School - JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Worcester Polytechnic Institute invites applications for the part-time position of Adjunct Teaching Faculty. The University welcomes culturally and academically diverse faculty of the highest caliber, skilled in the scholarship of teaching, discovery, application, and integration of knowledge. JOB DESCRIPTION Worcester Polytechnic Institute is seeking part-time adjunct faculty to teach day and/or evening classes for the WPI Business School at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Part-time faculty positions are created to provide an available applicant pool for the department. Positions will be filled on an as needed basis. Start dates will vary by course and semester. Qualified applicants will be contacted by the academic department when an opening is anticipated. Your application will remain in the pool until the posting closes. If you wish to be considered for positions beyond that date you must reapply. Responsibilities include teaching from established curriculum but with addition of personal expertise and experience; provide students with timely feedback; grading. Prior experience teaching college-level courses required. Applicants should review the WPI course catalog and identify Business School courses they may be qualified to teach. Experience with online teaching and the Canvas learning management system preferred but not required. PhD in related discipline preferred; master’s degree in related discipline required. Compensation: $6,000 per each undergraduate course and $6,500 per each graduate course. FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Assistant-Professor-of-Teaching_R0003832": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Teaching",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Professor of Teaching LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Integrative & Global Studies DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY WPI’s Department of Integrative and Global Studies seeks a full-time, tenure track Assistant Professor of Teaching, whose teaching, advising, and scholarly expertise will support WPI’s undergraduate interdisciplinary projects program. JOB DESCRIPTION About WPI   Worcester Polytechnic Institute was founded in 1865 to create and convey the latest science and engineering knowledge in ways that are most beneficial to society. Today, WPI holds firm to its founding mission to provide an education that balances theory with practice. WPI's 14 academic departments offer more than 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs in science, engineering, technology, business, the social sciences, and the humanities and arts, leading to bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degrees.   The university’s curriculum features a rigorous but flexible program that is project-based and globally-engaged, aimed at quality experience and positive engagement with communities around the world. WPI faculty members work with students on interdisciplinary research and design, addressing important and socially relevant challenges, both on campus and at more than 50 project centers throughout the Americas, Africa, Asia-Pacific, and Europe.  About the Department of Integrative and Global Studies   WPI’s Department of Integrative and Global Studies (DIGS) serves as a gateway for students and faculty to connect with and make a difference in communities around the world.  The department is the intellectual home of experiential and community-based learning at the university, providing stewardship for the university’s signature Global Projects Program . The Global Projects Program is an interdisciplinary, project-based learning initiative that engages students in collaborative, community-centered research and problem-solving with local and international partners to address real-world challenges. It offers a distinctive, interdisciplinary project experience that challenges students to address problems at the intersection of science, technology and society.   WPI requires students to work in teams to complete a nine-credit hour project that confronts an open-ended problem at the interface of society and technology, and most students fulfill this requirement through an 8-week term at one of the project centers around the world.  DIGS faculty members advise students through the full arc of these team-based projects. They teach a preparation course on research methods and design thinking; they travel with students and supervise projects at one of the university’s 50+ global project centers; and they help student process these experiences to advance career readiness and lifelong learning. In most cases, faculty co-advise these projects with other faculty members. The faculty also build on relationships at these global project centers to conduct research on topics such as sustainable food systems, urban planning, innovation and entrepreneurship, conservation, climate resilience, community development, and many other topics, including pedagogical research around project-based and community-engaged learning.   DIGS faculty also deliver the first-year project-based learning program, the Great Problems Seminar, which guides students through team-based projects addressing global grand challenges as part of a campus-based course.   About the Position   WPI’s Department of Integrative and Global Studies seeks a full-time, tenure track Assistant Professor of Teaching, whose teaching, advising, and scholarly expertise will support WPI’s undergraduate interdisciplinary projects program.    Principal Duties and Responsibilities   Successful candidates will prepare students for a nine-credit, team-based off-campus research project and design experience by teaching Social Science Research Methods and advising student projects at WPI’s off campus project centers for one or two 8-week terms per year.   Faculty responsibilities include classroom teaching, organizing and advising student research and design projects, and advancing high impact, innovative approaches to teaching and learning.  Over time, the successful candidate will be expected to take a leadership role in directing one of the off-campus project centers that comprise WPI’s domestic and global project network.  Successful candidates for this tenure track Assistant Professor of Teaching role are expected to be excellent teachers and project advisors, and active pedagogical innovators who develop and disseminate high impact teaching practices.  Faculty are also expected to participate in pre-departure training and orientation activities to ensure students are prepared to address health and safety issues while traveling , as well as department meetings and departmental service activities.     Desired Attributes   Experience teaching qualitative social science research methods and conducting  applied field research or community engagement.    Experience engaging students in off-campus experiential learning in diverse communities and organizational settings.    Demonstrated ability to collaborate with colleagues from a variety of disciplines.  Demonstrated evidence of flexibility, adaptability, and ability to pivot while guiding students.  Creativity in approaches to applied social science methods and collaborative design.  Strong interest in global engagement and experience engaging a wide variety of audiences including undergraduates, field professionals, scholars, policymakers, and other community stakeholders.  Experience with creative pedagogical approaches in the classroom.  Active engagement in workshops, conferences, and other professional communities.  Experience in relevant integrative settings, such as but not limited to, international field schools, experiential or project-based learning, cross-cultural engagement, non-profit management, foreign service, or corporate or public sector decision-making.  Demonstrated global and intercultural competency, such as through language or other cross cultural engagement skills.  Position Requirements   A PhD in a relevant integrative field, such as but not limited to, Anthropology, Geography, History, International Studies or Development, Psychology, Sociology, Education, Environmental and Sustainability Studies, Human-Centered Design, or Communication.   How to Apply   For full consideration, applications, including a cover letter (2 page max), CV, teaching statement (2 page max), research statement (2 page max), and contact information for references should be received by December 10, 2026.  Please note that we are also hiring a secure contract teaching faculty position in DIGS. Qualified applicants are welcome to submit applications for both positions. Compensation Salary: $82,500 - $85,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Assistant-Teaching-Professor_R0003833": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Teaching Professor LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Integrative & Global Studies DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY WPI’s Department of Integrative and Global Studies seeks a full-time, secure contract faculty at the Assistant Professor level whose teaching and project advising will support WPI’s undergraduate interdisciplinary projects program. Successful candidates will teach courses that prepare students for team-based, off-campus, including international, project work. The course address topics including social science research methods, effective communications and teamwork, ethical research and often includes material related to the students’ project country/location. In addition, assistant teaching professors will be required to travel with groups of students to advise team-based projects at WPI’s project centers around the world. Faculty also meet with student teams regularly during the term prior to travel to assist in the preparation of research proposals to guide the students’ work. JOB DESCRIPTION About WPI   Worcester Polytechnic Institute was founded in 1865 to create and convey the latest science and engineering knowledge in ways that are most beneficial to society. Today, WPI holds firm to its founding mission to provide an education that balances theory with practice. WPI's 14 academic departments offer more than 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs in science, engineering, technology, business, the social sciences, and the humanities and arts, leading to bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degrees.    The university’s curriculum features a rigorous but flexible program that is project-based and globally-engaged, aimed at quality experience and positive engagement with communities around the world. WPI faculty members work with students on interdisciplinary research and design, addressing important and socially relevant challenges, both on campus and at more than 50 project centers throughout the Americas, Africa, Asia-Pacific, and Europe.   About the Department of Integrative and Global Studies   WPI’s Department of Integrative and Global Studies ( DIGS) serves as a gateway for students and faculty to connect with and make a difference in communities around the world.  The department is the intellectual home of experiential and community-based learning at the university, providing stewardship for the university’s signature Global Projects Program. The Global Projects Program is an interdisciplinary, project-based learning initiative that engages students in collaborative, community-centered research and problem-solving with local and international partners to address real-world challenges. It offers a distinctive, interdisciplinary project experience that challenges students to address problems at the intersection of science, technology and society.    WPI requires students to work in teams to complete a nine-credit hour project that confronts an open-ended problem at the interface of society and technology, and most students fulfill this requirement through an 8-week term at one of the project centers around the world.       DIGS faculty members advise students through the full arc of these team-based projects. They teach a preparation course on research methods and design thinking; they travel with students and supervise projects at one of the university’s 50+ global project centers; and they help student process these experiences to advance career readiness and lifelong learning. In most cases, faculty co-advise these projects with other faculty members. The faculty also build on relationships at these global project centers to conduct research on topics such as sustainable food systems, urban planning, innovation and entrepreneurship, conservation, climate resilience, community development, and many other topics, including pedagogical research around project-based and community-engaged learning.   DIGS faculty also deliver the first-year project-based learning program, the Great Problems Seminar, which guides students through team-based projects addressing global grand challenges as part of a campus-based course.    Principal Duties and Responsibilities   Preparing students for their off-campus research and design experience by teaching Social Science Research Methods, which usually incorporates lecture, discussion, and active learning activities to guide students in background research, communication and developing clear, well-defined research plans for on-site work.     Advising student projects at WPI’s off campus project centers for one or two 8-week terms per year.  Faculty advisors support student teams throughout their project experience, providing guidance on research, project development, and professional collaboration. Advisors also review student writing, help teams navigate team dynamics, and address health and safety issues as needed.   Over time, the successful candidate may have the opportunity to take a leadership role in directing one of the off-campus project centers that comprise WPI’s domestic and global project network.   Faculty also participate in pre-departure training and orientation activities to ensure students are prepared to address health and safety issues while traveling, as well as department meetings and departmental service activities.   Desired Attributes   Experience teaching qualitative social science research methods and conducting  applied field research or community engagement.     Experience engaging students in off-campus experiential learning in diverse communities and organizational settings.     Demonstrated ability to collaborate with colleagues from a variety of disciplines.   Demonstrated evidence of flexibility, adaptability, and ability to pivot while guiding students.   Creativity in teaching applied social science methods and collaborative design.   Strong interest in global engagement and experience engaging a wide variety of audiences including undergraduates, field professionals, scholars, policymakers, and other community stakeholders.   Experience in relevant integrative settings, such as but not limited to, international field schools, experiential or project-based learning, cross-cultural engagement, foreign service, non-profit management, or corporate or public sector decision making.   Demonstrated global and intercultural competency, such as through language or other cross cultural engagement skills.   Position Requirements   A PhD in a relevant integrative field, such as but not limited to, Anthropology, Geography, History, International Studies or Development, Psychology, Sociology, Education, Environmental and Sustainability Studies, Human-Centered Design, or Communication.    How to Apply   For full consideration, applications, including a cover letter (2 page max), CV, teaching statement (2 page max), and research statement (2 page max), and contact information for references should be received by December 10, 2026.  Please note that we are also hiring a tenure track teaching faculty position in DIGS. Qualified applicants are welcome to submit applications for both positions. Compensation Salary: $82,500 - $85,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Post-Doctoral-Fellow_R0003825": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Post-Doctoral Fellow LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Mechanical & Materials Engineering - NFR JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Computational Multiphase Transport Laboratory (CMTL) at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position in computational multiphase flow. JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities: The successful candidate will work on high-fidelity computational modeling of droplet dynamics, droplet–droplet interactions, and droplet interactions with solid surfaces, with particular emphasis on applications relevant to aircraft and wind-turbine icing. Research topics may include droplet impact, deformation, coalescence, breakup, splashing, heat transfer, phase change/freezing, and the interaction of multiple droplets with cold surfaces. The project will involve development and application of advanced numerical methods for interfacial multiphase flows, analysis of the governing physical mechanisms, and comparison with available experimental data. Qualifications: Applicants should have: A Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, Applied Mathematics, Computational Science, or a closely related field. A strong background in computational fluid dynamics and multiphase/interfacial flows. Experience with numerical modeling of droplets, free-surface flows, phase change, heat transfer, or related transport phenomena. Strong programming and scientific computing skills. A record of peer-reviewed publications and the ability to conduct independent research and communicate results effectively. Experience with OpenFOAM and/or Basilisk is highly desirable. Experience with interface-capturing methods such as VOF, adaptive mesh refinement, high- performance computing, or simulations involving droplet impact and solidification/freezing would also be advantageous. The postdoctoral researcher will have opportunities to contribute to model development, conduct large-scale numerical simulations, collaborate with experimental and computational researchers, publish in leading journals, and mentor graduate and undergraduate students. To Apply: Please include a cover letter and CV and contact information for at least two references Compensation: Salary: $60,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Post-Doctoral-Fellow--WUI-Fire-Emissions--Experiments--and-Multiscale-Modeling-_R0003822": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow (WUI Fire Emissions, Experiments, and Multiscale Modeling)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Post Doctoral Fellow (WUI Fire Emissions, Experiments, and Multiscale Modeling) LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Civil, Environmental, & Architectural Engineering - NFR JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The research group at Worcester Polytechnic Institute is seeking a postdoctoral researcher to contribute to an interdisciplinary project on wildland-urban interface (WUI) fire emissions from building materials and scaled residential structures. The primary focus of the position is to support the current project through a combination of experiments, data analysis, and modeling. The project examines how fire behavior influences aerosol and gas emissions, whether emissions from complex building assemblies can be predicted from their individual materials, and how fire behavior and emission characteristics change across experimental scales. The position is intended for a researcher who can contribute strongly to the current project while developing broader expertise in multiscale wildfire-smoke research. • PRIMARY FOCUS: Current WUI fire-emissions project funded by NSF: scaled house construction, multiscale experiments, data analysis, and model-supported interpretation. • GROWTH DIRECTION: Extend project findings toward multiscale wildfire-smoke modeling, smoke transport, and interactions with the built environment. JOB DESCRIPTION Research Responsibilities • Contribute to the planning, execution, and analysis of medium- and large-scale WUI fire experiments at WPI using building materials and scaled residential building models. • Analyze integrated fire-behavior and emission datasets, including temperature, heat flux, flow/velocity, combustion indicators, and aerosol/gas measurements collected with project collaborators. • Evaluate fire behavior-emission relationships, emission additivity, and scale dependence across bench-, medium-, and large-scale experiments, with attention to uncertainty and reproducibility. • Develop or apply computational models that help interpret the experiments and investigate transfer of information across scales. Depending on expertise, this may involve fire/smoke CFD, reduced-order or physics informed models, urban dispersion tools, or multiscale model coupling. • Prepare peer-reviewed manuscripts and conference presentations, collaborate with graduate students and project partners, and contribute to proposal development and preliminary studies related to future wildfire smoke research. Qualifications Applicants should hold a PhD in mechanical engineering, architectural/building engineering, fire protection engineering, civil/environmental engineering, chemical engineering, atmospheric science, or a closely related field. Strong candidates will demonstrate: • Research experience in fire, smoke, thermal-fluid sciences, pollutant transport, or a closely related area. • A strong foundation in heat and mass transfer, fluid mechanics, combustion, or environmental transport, together with scientific programming and quantitative data-analysis skills. • Experience with experimental research or experimental datasets; candidates who combine computational modeling with hands-on experimental experience are especially encouraged. • A peer-reviewed publication record and the ability to carry research from analysis and interpretation through manuscript preparation. Additional Relevant Experience Experience with medium- or large-scale fire experiments, aerosol/gas measurements, building-material combustion, model validation, uncertainty quantification, high-performance computing, or tools such as FDS/WFDS, QUIC-Fire/QUIC-SMOKE, FIRETEC, or related fire/dispersion models is desirable but not required. Application To apply, please include - A current Curriculum Vitae (CV) - One representative peer-reviewed publication that best reflects the candidate’s research experience and is closely aligned with the research direction of this position Inquires should be sent to Dr. Shichao Liu at sliu8@wpi.edu: Please use the subject line “Postdoctoral Applicant – WUI Fire Smoke”. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Compensation Salary: $55,000.00 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Administrative-Associate_R0003816": {
      "title": "Administrative Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Administrative Associate LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Physics DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY As part of the physics departmental team, provide administrative support to the physics department faculty, staff, and students. This role involves managing the departmental office's daily operations, coordinating events, communicating with stakeholders, and supporting the academic programs. JOB DESCRIPTION Responsibilities: Assist with program materials: Responsible for collecting course materials provided by others, including syllabi, examinations, and study guides, and for entering and maintaining course material on the web or other archives. Provide previous syllabi upon request. Self-Study, accreditation, ABET assessment: Maintain and organize all course materials obtained from professors for presentation to accreditation teams and assessment studies; assist with assessment data collection and record keeping; maintain and update the department library and archives in coordination with faculty. Assists with daily operations: Work on various departmental office and support activities (letters, memos, reports, newsletters, calendars…) using Word, Excel, Outlook, Teams, Qualtrics, Google Forms, MailChimp, etc. Maintain and update department web pages, multimedia outlets (electronic bulletin boards), and Learning Management Sites. Create and update department literature brochures in print and for electronic/online dissemination; Maintain and update department display cases, bulletin boards, and directory. ​ Graduate Program Support: Work closely with the Departmental Graduate Committee; coordinate with the Graduate School; organize and record graduate committee meetings; monitor and update graduate student records; oversee the graduate student application process, correspondence, and information requests; assist with graduate student activities around milestones, such as helping develop, deliver, and record the program progress. Events Coordinator: Oversee the planning, coordination, and execution of departmental and some university events (as appropriate), including colloquia, seminars, Undergraduate Research Projects Showcase Day, Sigma Xi, Society of Physics Students, Sigma Pi Sigma, and open houses; Responsibilities include crafting and disseminating event announcements via WPI Today and email, coordinating with other offices, managing registration, venue setups, and refreshments; Supporting department outreach and special projects to enhance community engagement and visibility. Assist with Departmental Daily Operations: Serve as a receptionist; answer inquiries (phone, email, in-person, Zoom) from faculty, students, and staff by responding to routine/specific inquiries or referring to appropriate staff members; greet visitors and interact with students and prospective students to help meet their informational needs; supervise work-study students and their biweekly payrolls; Take meeting minutes at departmental committee meetings; Order and keep the office supply inventory. Academic Operations: Coordinates with and assists other departmental staff and faculty with the TA and PLA scheduling and coverage of the activities to support the teaching mission and other requests to support the department's research and outreach missions; reserves rooms for exams and study sessions, and coordinates TAs for proctoring exams. Requirements: High School Diploma. 0-3 years of relevant experience. Compensation: Salary: $21.25 - $23.00 per hour.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Non-Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Institutional-Research-Analyst_R0003817": {
      "title": "Institutional Research Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Institutional Research Analyst LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Institutional Research, Analytics & Assessment DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Institutional Research Analyst supports data-informed decision-making through dashboard development, institutional analysis, and higher education market research. The position transforms data from multiple sources into reliable, accessible, and actionable insights to support institutional planning, assessment, enrollment strategies, and academic program development. The Analyst develops and maintains analytical products, interprets institutional and market data, and communicates findings through clear visualizations, reports, and presentations. Working collaboratively with campus stakeholders, the Analyst helps improve data quality, accessibility, and data literacy to inform institutional planning and decision-making. The Analyst is part of a dynamic, collaborative team that values curiosity, continuous improvement, and shared problem-solving. The ideal candidate is comfortable navigating ambiguity, balancing competing priorities, and developing practical solutions to evolving analytical needs. JOB DESCRIPTION 1. Dashboard Development, Data Visualization, and Data Management Develop and maintain interactive dashboards, visualizations, and reporting tools that support institutional planning, assessment, and decision-making. Prepare, integrate, and validate data from multiple sources, improving accuracy, reliability, and efficiency through repeatable and automated processes. Translate stakeholder needs into effective reporting solutions and document data sources, definitions, and processes to support consistent interpretation and data quality. 2. Institutional Analysis and Market Research Conduct analyses of institutional data related to students, enrollment, academic programs, research, institutional effectiveness, and strategic priorities. Research higher education trends, peer institutions, demographics, academic programs, and market conditions to identify opportunities and inform enrollment strategies and institutional planning. Communicate findings through clear reports, presentations, and other analytical products that support decision-making by university leadership and campus stakeholders. 3. Collaboration, Process Improvement, and Data Literacy Collaborate with academic departments and administrative teams on analytical projects and initiatives that improve access to and effective use of institutional information. Identify opportunities to streamline reporting processes, improve operational efficiency, and enhance data accessibility and service delivery. Develop documentation, quick-reference guides, infographics, newsletters, and training materials that promote data literacy and awareness of institutional research resources. Other duties may be assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in a relevant field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. At least one year of relevant experience in institutional research, data analytics, business intelligence, higher education research, or a related area. Experience preparing, cleaning, validating, and analyzing data from multiple sources and developing dashboards, reports, or data visualizations. Strong analytical, critical-thinking, and problem-solving skills, including the ability to identify data issues, interpret findings, and work through ambiguous or open-ended questions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to explain complex information to nontechnical audiences. Strong attention to detail and data quality, along with the ability to manage multiple assignments, meet deadlines, and work independently and collaboratively. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in higher education, social science, data analytics, statistics, public policy, business, or a related field. Experience working in institutional research or higher education. Experience with Power BI or a comparable data visualization platform, including data modeling, DAX, Power Query, or automated data preparation. Experience with R, Python, SPSS, or comparable statistical or programming software. Salary Grade 8: $72,00 – $76,000 per year (depending on experience)   Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.    Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Operations-Coordinator_R0003829": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Operations Coordinator LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME STEM Education DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The STEM Education Center endeavors to transform STEM education to be meaningful, inclusive, and impactful through PreK-12 Teacher Education. The Center's Operations Coordinator manages the daily operations of the Center and is the primary contact to stakeholders and partners who engage with the Center. They provide administrative, marketing, and budgetary/financial support for the Center's operations. In addition, the Operations Coordinator serves as the program manager for the Central Massachusetts STEM Network (CMSN) Ecosystem, as well as supporting other growth areas of the Center. This is a full-time, on-site, fixed-term position funded through June 30, 2027, with the possibility of extension based on continued funding. Some flexibility in work hours and days may be possible, as long as the 9 a.m. to 3 p.m. business hours are covered. JOB DESCRIPTION Manages the Center's Daily Office Operations and Records - Provides comprehensive administrative support for the STEM Education Center and its team members, working closely with the Executive Director - Manages the daily operations and administrative functions of the Center (e.g., responds to Center email and phone inquiries, orders supplies, works with IT and facilities as needed) - Coordinates and schedules meetings, makes room reservations, and drafts and disseminates communications to the Center team regarding deadlines, updates, new opportunities, and WPI policy and procedure changes - Maintains a welcoming and supportive office environment for Center members, students, and guests - Recruits, trains, and supervises Center student assistants; manages timesheets, payroll, costing allocations, and student worker schedules Oversees Purchasing, Finances, and Accounts Payable - Monitors Center accounts (including select grants) monthly and rectifies discrepancies through financial processes aligned with university policies - Supports team members in creating and monitoring customer invoices in Workday (for school PDs, out-of-school time partners, and others) - Supports team members in submitting supplier invoices and supporting documents in Workday (for consultants, Teacher Prep mentor teachers, and program supervisors) and monitors for payment - Oversees the process of administering and maintaining contracts - Creates budget documents and analyses to support the Center's budget planning and reporting Manages Center Communications, Marketing, and Events - Supports Center-wide communications by producing monthly newsletters, maintaining Center email lists and the CMSN Ecosystem member database, promoting the Center, overseeing promotional materials, and preparing for conferences and literature tables at events - Creates and updates department websites using content management systems such as Drupal and WordPress, and maintains signage, announcements, and displays - Coordinates Center activities and events that host participants or guests, including venue reservations, room preparations, catering requests, visitor directions and transportation information, parking permits, technology setup, breakdown, and cleaning requests - Collaborates with multiple campus stakeholders Qualifications - Associate's degree required; Bachelor's degree preferred - At least three years of relevant office work experience - Strong ability to manage, plan, and prioritize multiple tasks with attention to detail in a fast-paced environment - Excellent written and verbal communication skills - Competency with office and communication tools (e.g., Microsoft Office, Google Suite) and willingness to learn new systems - Experience with Workday and payroll systems preferred - Demonstrated skills in managing, analyzing, and projecting budgets and producing reports - Ability to create flyers, newsletters, announcements, and webpages - Ability to work independently and as part of a team, with a high level of professionalism and discretion Compensation : $49,500 - $65,000 per year (depending on experience). Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.    Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Visiting-Assistant-Professor_R0003826": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Visiting Assistant Professor LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Mechanical & Materials Engineering DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY The Mechanical and Materials Engineering Department at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI) seeks applicants for a short-term, non-tenure-track faculty position in the area of Applied Artificial Intelligence (AI) for Mechatronics and Controls available at the visiting assistant professor level. This position is in addition to our tenure-track search, position R0003517. The successful candidate will possess a strong background in Mechatronics and Controls, and demonstrate proficiency in applying AI techniques to these application areas. Expertise in intelligent sensing and perception, cyber-physical systems, data-driven modeling and control, and AI/deep learning is essential. Applications may include autonomous systems and mobility, intelligent infrastructure, human–robot interaction, smart health, advanced manufacturing and Industry 4.0/5.0, mechatronics for sustainability, and other areas complementary to the department’s existing strengths. A proven ability to integrate multidisciplinary methodologies to solve complex challenges is highly desirable. The candidate will be expected to develop an active externally funded research program and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in mechanical engineering. Candidate is required to have a PhD or equivalent degree in mechanical engineering, robotics engineering, or another relevant discipline. This position is expected to be filled at the Visting Assistant Professor level. JOB DESCRIPTION The Mechanical and Materials Engineering Department at WPI (https://www.wpi.edu/academics/departments/mechanical-materials-engineering) has 27 dual-mission faculty, 9 full-time teaching faculty, and 12 research faculty.  MME offers undergraduate degrees in mechanical engineering to over 1100 students. The department offers graduate degrees in mechanical engineering, materials science and engineering, and manufacturing engineering to about 200 graduate students. The department’s annual externally sponsored research expenditures have exceeded $16M. WPI’s reputation as a rigorous and innovative university rests on the shoulders of its faculty. A highly selective, private technological university and one of the nation’s first, WPI believes that when great minds work together, great advances follow. At WPI the boundaries to multidisciplinary collaboration are low—faculty members, students, and other partners work together on the real-world projects and purposeful research that are hallmarks of the WPI experience. Located one hour west of Boston, the university’s campus is in Worcester, Massachusetts, a thriving 21st century college city recognized as a growing hub of scientific and technological innovation. Applications should include curriculum vitae, statements of teaching and research interests, and a list of five professional references. This search will remain open until the position is filled. Questions can be addressed to the Chair of the Search Committee, Professor Zhu Mao, zmao2@wpi.edu. Compensation: Salary: $105,000 - $108,000 per year.  Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity.  WPI’s benefits package includes a robust retirement match, wellness perks, tuition assistance and more! FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Temporary-Payroll-Specialist--20-hours-week-_R0003824": {
      "title": "Temporary Payroll Specialist (20 hours/week)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nJOB TITLE Temporary Payroll Specialist (20 hours/week) LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Payroll - JM DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Reporting to the Associate Director, the Temporary Payroll Specialist works approximately 20 hours per week to support the daily operations of the payroll function, including customer service and end-to-end processing of multiple biweekly payrolls in Workday HCM, to ensure accurate and timely payment to all employees. The role processes regular payroll as well as special payments such as net pay reissues and other out-of-sequence payrolls. The Temporary Payroll Specialist reviews and analyzes employee transactions and time reports to ensure compliance with federal and state regulations, university policy, the employee handbook, and provisions of collective bargaining agreements. This is a temporary position of approximately six months, with possible extension, at an hourly rate of $30–$35, intended to provide additional support to the payroll team. JOB DESCRIPTION Payroll Processing Assist with biweekly payroll processing for multiple pay groups, including auditing, analyzing, verifying, and entering payroll items. Ensure accuracy of gross-to-net calculations, benefit deductions, retirement contributions, tax withholding, union dues, and garnishments. Review reported time, follow up with managers and employees, evaluate time and adjustments from prior pay periods, evaluate unprocessed time, and clean up unsubmitted time entries. Run payroll audit reports in preparation for processing, follow up as needed, and make adjustments and fixes for accurate and timely payment. Communicate about missing direct deposit information, distribution of printed checks or pay cards, net pay reissues, direct deposit rejects, and stale-dated checks. Customer Service Communicate via email, phone, and videoconference with employees, departments, managers, and agencies, researching and responding to questions about pay, deductions, taxes, etc. Manage the payroll queue in the Halo ticketing system. Send general reminders related to time entry and approval deadlines, year-end processing, etc. Create and update procedure and job-aid documentation for department and community use. Maintain accurate information on the website and in Workday, including setting up processing for future payrolls and vacation and holiday calendars. Payroll Knowledge, Reporting, Tax Filing & Reconciliation Support compliance with federal, state, and local payroll laws and regulations, including wage and hour, tax, and reporting requirements. Develop a strong understanding of collective bargaining agreements, including time entry codes, pay rules, earning calculations, and reporting. Support calendar year-end processes, including W-2 preparation, reconciliation, and distribution. Run reports to assist with preparing periodic, quarterly, and annual tax filings. Respond to audit requests and compliance reporting. Assist with entries to finalize fiscal year-end. Track garnishments and advance repayments, and create accounts payable requisitions. Assist with registering and maintaining payroll tax accounts, reconciling ADP tax transactions, filing and paying tax obligations outside of ADP’s processing, and reconciling payroll tax records with ADP, tax agency, and accounting records. Testing & System Maintenance Partner with internal and external teams for Workday testing related to system projects, optimization, enhancements, fixes, and new releases. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Required Bachelor’s degree or equivalent combination of education and experience. 2+ years of payroll experience, including Workday HCM payroll. Knowledge of federal and state payroll laws and regulations. Proficiency in Excel. Excellent customer service and communication skills with an aptitude for problem-solving. Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and meet payroll deadlines. Availability to work approximately 20 hours per week remotely, including coverage on biweekly payroll deadline days. Preferred Experience with ADP payroll tax processing and reconciliation. Experience in a unionized environment and applying collective bargaining agreement pay rules. Payroll experience in higher education. Additional Details : This is a temporary position of approximately six months, with possible extension, at an hourly rate of $30–$35, intended to provide additional support to the payroll team. FLSA STATUS United States of America (Non-Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Assistant-Director--Learning---Development_R0003818": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Learning & Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Assistant Director, Learning & Development LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Talent Strategies DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Reporting to the Senior Director of Talent, the Assistant Director of Learning & Development advances learning and development programs across WPI and champions a culture of continuous learning for faculty and staff. The role designs, delivers, and evaluates university-wide initiatives, including learning pathways, leadership and manager development, onboarding curriculum, and professional development opportunities, that strengthen organizational effectiveness and drive individual and institutional performance. As a trusted advisor and strategic partner to campus leaders and Talent Strategy Partners (TSPs), the Assistant Director brings expertise in facilitation, program design, and data-informed evaluation, and manages the University’s learning management system and related technologies. This work improves employee learning, engagement, retention, and belonging across the WPI community. JOB DESCRIPTION Learning Program Design, Delivery & Facilitation: Design, develop, and deliver workshops, learning series, cohort-based programs, and learning pathways, in person and online, that support career growth for faculty and staff. Partner with senior leaders, TSPs, and academic and administrative leaders to assess learning needs and develop a forward-looking L&D strategy that builds critical skills for current and future needs. Facilitate engaging sessions for faculty, staff, managers, and senior leadership; train internal facilitators and subject matter experts as appropriate. Stay current on adult learning best practices and AI-enabled learning technologies to evolve program offerings. Leadership & Manager Development: Design and facilitate leadership and manager development programs that strengthen coaching, communication, and supervisory effectiveness, including programs for new supervisors, emerging leaders, and faculty in leadership roles. Create tools and resources that support performance management, feedback conversations, and career development. Talent & Organizational Development: Manage a portfolio of talent programs, including succession planning and performance management. Partner with Talent & Inclusion (T&I) to develop onboarding curriculum and orientation resources. Collaborate with T&I and divisional partners on talent management and retention strategies, including recognition and career development. Support organizational development initiatives, including change management and team effectiveness. Data, Analytics & Program Evaluation: Conduct needs assessments and gap analyses to prioritize development opportunities and inform talent strategy. Track and report on program effectiveness, participation, and impact; use findings and feedback to improve programs. Design, administer, and analyze employee engagement and pulse surveys; translate results into dashboards, executive summaries, and recommendations. Continuous Learning Culture, Engagement & Inclusion: Promote a culture of continuous learning by increasing awareness of and participation in professional and career development opportunities. Establish and support communities of practice that encourage collaboration and knowledge sharing among faculty and staff. Partner with T&I and organizational leaders to embed belonging and equity principles into L&D programming and talent practices. Learning Technology & Required Learning: Administer the University’s learning management system and related technologies, including course administration, governance, and user support. Curate content and manage digital learning libraries so employees can easily find resources. Coordinate required and compliance training with content owners across the University, including assigning, tracking, and reporting on completion. Operational & Project Support: Coordinate scheduling, logistics, and communication for learning initiatives and events. Manage external vendors, facilitators, and consultants, including contracts, invoicing, and performance expectations. Maintain program documentation and contribute to process improvements across HR and L&D. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in Organizational Development, Human Resources, Education, or a related field. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in learning and development, talent or organizational development, training, human resources, higher education administration, or a related field. Experience designing, implementing, and facilitating employee development programs. Experience with learning management systems or learning technologies (e.g., LinkedIn Learning, Workday, Vector Solutions). Strong project management skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and timelines. Ability to maintain confidentiality, discretion, and professionalism in an HR environment. Excellent communication and stakeholder management skills with the ability to influence at all levels. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in Organizational Development, Adult Learning, Human Resources, or a related field. Experience supporting leadership development and manager effectiveness programs. ATD (CPTD/CPLP), SHRM-CP/SCP, or equivalent certification. Experience with AI-enabled learning tools and instructional technologies. Background in higher education or a similarly complex institutional environment. Familiarity with Workday or other HCM systems and with behavioral/leadership assessment tools. Salary Grade 8: $71,800– $90,000 per year (depending on experience)   Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.    Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    },
    "https://wpi.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WPI_External_Career_Site/job/Worcester/Senior-Director--Talent-Strategies---Workforce-Planning_R0003819": {
      "title": "Senior Director, Talent Strategies & Workforce Planning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nJOB TITLE Senior Director, Talent Strategies & Workforce Planning LOCATION Worcester DEPARTMENT NAME Talent & Inclusion Division DIVISION NAME Worcester Polytechnic Institute - WPI JOB DESCRIPTION SUMMARY Reporting to the Vice President of Talent & Chief Human Resources Officer (CHRO), the Senior Director, Talent Strategies & Workforce Planning serves as a senior advisor to institutional leaders and leads the Talent Strategy Partner (TSP) team, including two Senior TSPs and a Talent Specialist, in delivering consultative Talent & Inclusion (T&I) solutions to faculty, staff, and union-represented employees across the University. The Senior Director partners with senior leaders to understand unit goals and challenges and to align integrated T&I solutions with institutional strategy, providing guidance on workforce strategy, organizational design, talent planning, and change management in a complex, unionized higher education environment. In addition to leading the team, the role carries its own portfolio of assigned departments and serves as the escalation point for complex employee and labor relations matters, ensuring consistent application of university policies, collective bargaining agreements, and legal requirements. The role coaches and develops TSPs as strategic consultants and drives continuous improvement in the quality and consistency of T&I service delivery. JOB DESCRIPTION Strategic Advisory & Workforce Planning Serve as a senior advisor to assigned deans, department heads, and administrative leaders, aligning integrated T&I solutions with institutional strategy and providing guidance on workforce strategy, organizational design, talent planning, and change management. Partner with units to develop and implement workforce and succession plans that anticipate current and future talent needs. Facilitate talent reviews with leadership teams, assess leadership bench strength with Learning & Development, and identify development opportunities. Analyze workforce, turnover, and talent data to identify trends and barriers to organizational success and recommend proactive solutions that improve organizational effectiveness. Promote a culture of inclusion, accountability, engagement, and continuous improvement across assigned areas. Team Leadership & Development Lead, coach, and develop two Senior TSPs and a Talent Specialist, setting priorities, expectations, and performance goals, and developing TSPs as strategic consultants through case consultation and professional development. Hire, onboard, and evaluate team members. Provide direction, feedback, and support to ensure consistency, quality, and impact of TSP service delivery across the University, including consistent practices, tools, and standards. Assign departments and manage workload and coverage for portfolios and strategic initiatives. Oversee the Talent Specialist's work on front desk operations, I-9 compliance, and administrative support to the TSP team, ensuring timely, accurate, and compliant processes. Portfolio Management & Talent Partnership Serve as the primary T&I point of contact for an assigned portfolio of departments, providing consultative guidance to leaders, managers, faculty, and staff, including units with union-represented employees. Develop and execute T&I strategic plans for assigned departments with a focus on talent acquisition, performance management, culture and engagement, and retention initiatives. Partner with Talent Acquisition to develop strategic recruitment plans and with Learning & Development to identify departmental learning needs and develop leadership, manager, and continuous learning opportunities. Build strong working relationships with assigned departments through regular check-ins with leaders, managers, faculty, and staff. Employee & Labor Relations and Policy Compliance Lead and support the TSP team in managing employee relations, including performance management, corrective action, and workplace concerns, with a proactive, solutions-focused approach and consistent, effective guidance to supervisors and employees; serve as the escalation point for complex or high-risk matters, guiding investigations and resolutions. Advise senior leaders on conflict navigation and institutional response strategies for sensitive or high-risk matters. Collaborate with divisional and university leadership and the Office of General Counsel to resolve conflicts and provide counseling, mediation, support, and advice. Advise academic leaders on faculty employment matters in accordance with applicable faculty governance procedures and policies. Partner with Labor & Talent Programs and union representatives on collective bargaining agreements, formal grievances, and union engagement, ensuring employee relations actions align with contractual obligations. Ensure consistent interpretation and application of university policies, procedures, collective bargaining agreements, and legal requirements to mitigate organizational risk. Serve as the university T&I representative with external agencies regarding employee-related activities, such as the EEOC, human rights, unemployment, and the Department of Labor. Organizational Design & Change Management Lead or advise on organizational assessments, restructurings, and change initiatives, keeping them aligned with institutional priorities and financially sustainable. Provide guidance on job architecture, promotions, organizational structure, job redesigns, spans of control, and reporting structures to improve operational effectiveness. T&I Program Implementation & Cross-Functional Collaboration Serve as a liaison between the TSP team and other T&I teams to ensure seamless implementation of T&I programs, such as performance management, engagement surveys, and merit increases. Track trends and program impact and recommend improvements to T&I programs and policies informed by stakeholder feedback and institutional needs. Ensure information in the Human Resource Information System (HRIS) is accurate and approved in a timely manner across the TSP team. Contribute to T&I leadership planning, goals, and reporting on team outcomes. Performs all other duties and responsibilities as assigned or directed by the supervisor. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Human Resources, Organizational Development, Business Administration, or a related field. 10+ years of progressive HR experience, including advanced employee and/or labor relations experience in a highly complex organizational environment. At least five (5) years of people leadership or team management experience. Executive-level experience leading resolution strategies for high-risk or sensitive employee and/or labor relations matters and advising senior leaders on organizational risk and conflict navigation. Demonstrated experience as a strategic HR business partner or HR leader advising senior-level clients. Strong knowledge of workforce and succession planning, employee relations, performance management, organizational design, labor laws, and collective bargaining agreements, with experience navigating a complex, matrixed, or unionized environment. Strategic thinking with a proven ability to lead change and align T&I services with organizational goals. Exceptional communication, critical thinking, conflict resolution, and facilitation skills. Sound judgment and the ability to build relationships and influence faculty, staff, union representatives, and leaders at all levels. Ability to handle confidential and sensitive information with discretion. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree in Human Resources, Labor Relations, Public Administration, Business, or a related field. Higher education or public-sector experience, including familiarity with faculty governance and academic personnel processes. Experience interpreting and applying collective bargaining agreements and labor relations practices. Experience with job architecture, workforce analytics, and conducting workplace investigations. Active SHRM-CP, SHRM-SCP, PHR, or SPHR certification. Salary Grade 12: $127,500 – $153,000 per year (depending on experience)   Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range.    Compensation at WPI FLSA STATUS United States of America (Exempt) WPI is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran status, or disability. It seeks individuals from all backgrounds and experiences who will contribute to a culture of creativity, collaboration, inclusion, problem solving, innovation, high performance, and change making. It is committed to maintaining a campus environment free of harassment and discrimination."
    }
  },
